    {
      "content": "{\"role\": \"B\", \"goal\": \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", \"experiment\": \"8ab810087a9045b5-h01\", \"ordinal\": 2, \"integration_source_ids\": [], \"current_source_paths\": [\"src/model-view/primetest.c\", \"src/sources/primetest.c\"], \"context_as_data\": {\"sources\": [], \"interactions\": [], \"automatic_observations\": {\"baseline\": {\"experiment_id\": \"baseline-e1\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.5, \"mean_seconds\": 0.04357344564050436}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_parent\": {\"experiment_id\": \"802481162cb645ba\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"A 30-wheel integer-only classifier will remove floating-point work from trial division, while precomputing fragments and forming each candidate row in an independent contiguous loop will expose a legal SIMD opportunity under GCC on ARM64.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.8843928152266095, \"mean_seconds\": 0.005695889083047708}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_base_commit\": \"b220693cfd57ce5307c694636af180ac9b0f466b\", \"recent_own_role\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"802481162cb645ba\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"A 30-wheel integer-only classifier will remove floating-point work from trial division, while precomputing fragments and forming each candidate row in an independent contiguous loop will expose a legal SIMD opportunity under GCC on ARM64.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.8843928152266095, \"mean_seconds\": 0.005695889083047708}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}], \"own_role_history\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"802481162cb645ba\", \"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.8843928152266095, \"mean_seconds\": 0.005695889083047708}], \"node_development\": {\"attempt\": 1, \"remaining_attempts\": 3, \"previous_attempts\": [], \"confirmation_data_available\": false}}, \"documents_as_data\": [], \"deadline\": 1792278620.4095676, \"generation_deadline\": 1792278620.4095676, \"protocol_id\": \"6cf17cb49563821e287cce915cf056d9ccc39dfe3f7c1e38fbb38910bfd2e400\", \"remaining_tokens\": 589824, \"official_statement_as_data\": \"Problem E\\nYet More Primes\\nPrime test is a common task in many applications, specially for security, cryptography,\\nand other interest areas. There are a number of techniques used to verify if a number is\\nprime, and the common problem is that all techniques consumes lots of time.\\nThe problem is easy to solve, giving the number to be tested and time enough to calculate.\\nBut a weird scientist took our numbers, and cut them in two halves, so we have ﬁrst to\\nre-arrange the numbers, looking for the primes. In this case, we have the certainty that all\\nthe numbers we have are primes, but the problem is how to re-order them\\nInput\\nIn the ﬁrst line, there is the numberP of prime numbers (1≤ P≤ 1000). Then, 2P lines\\nwith parts of the numbers to be tested, with no order. Prime numbers in this problem are\\ntaken as strings, so ﬁrst half and second half simply means an arbitrary cut of such string.\\nFor instance, prime number 504155039 can be cut in 5041 and 55039, or 504155 and 039\\nNote that second half numbers could begin with a lead zero, so you must take it into\\naccount. Of course, this doesn’t happen with the ﬁrst half.\\nThe input must be read from the standard input.\\nOutput\\nYou have to print the list of the P prime numbers, in ascendant order.\\nThe output must be written to the standard output.\\nExample\\nInput Output\\n4 1000213\\n50415 5041501\\n100 5575001\\n5041 504155039\\n55750 504155713\\n5039\\n01\\n0213\\n55713\\n\\n\\nThis experiment uses a reviewed finite certified input/output contract only. There are six private research cases and six distinct private confirmation cases. P is in {1,2,4,8,16,30}; the input lists two separately permuted groups of P decimal fragments. Left fragments have no leading zero and end in an even digit; right fragments have seven characters including a leading zero. Each original pair comes from a true prime and the multiset of every admissible prime concatenation equals the P source occurrences. Concatenations are at most 600971707; output is compared as exact bytes. The official example has an unresolved P/output-count contradiction. No claim is made for the whole contest domain or arbitrary fragment orders. The fixed benchmark is one member of the same certified finite set; no hidden values or expected outputs are supplied. Target: Linux ARM64, four CPU cores, 16 GiB RAM, zero GPUs. Compilation: GCC -O2 -std=c11 -Werror=uninitialized -Werror=maybe-uninitialized -lm.\", \"runtime_as_data\": {\"cpu_cores\": 4, \"OMP_NUM_THREADS\": 4, \"architecture\": \"ARM64\", \"candidate_GPU\": false, \"comparison\": \"Exact output bytes against official reference\"}, \"mother_model_as_data\": {\"phase\": \"preparation\", \"status\": \"complete\", \"guidance\": \"Research an integer-only wheel trial-division classifier as an independent alternative. Handle n < 2 and small prime factors, then test only 30-wheel candidate divisors using d <= n/d. Avoid sqrtl and floating-point fmod, and preserve the existing candidate multiset and output behavior. Measure it independently against baseline; do not combine it with another primality engine without measuring the combination.\", \"analysis\": \"The source tests P² concatenations (at most 900 under this contract), then repeatedly builds strings and calls isprime, which uses sqrtl and floating-point fmod while checking every factor. The observed baseline is correct but has room for performance work; there is no profiling evidence establishing which alternative will win, so A/B/C should test distinct classifiers independently. The certified candidate bound permits a bounded deterministic Miller–Rabin experiment, while a shared sieve and integer wheel division provide separate alternatives. Preserve general computation for the stated contract rather than encoding sample or private-case answers. The official example’s output-count contradiction is unresolved; do not use it to infer behavior beyond the reviewed contract.\", \"origin\": \"model output from this fresh run; no operator analysis\"}}, \"required_json_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"hypothesis\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"transformations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"sources_kept\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"uniqueItems\": true, \"maxItems\": 0}, \"files\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"content\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"content\"]}}, \"notes\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"hypothesis\", \"transformations\", \"sources_kept\", \"files\", \"notes\"]}}\n\nCURRENT SOURCE AS DATA:\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n  static const int increments[8] = {4, 2, 4, 2, 4, 6, 2, 6};\n  long int factor=7;\n  int step=0;\n\n  if (value < 2)\n    return 0;\n  if (value == 2 || value == 3 || value == 5)\n    return 1;\n  if (value % 2 == 0 || value % 3 == 0 || value % 5 == 0)\n    return 0;\n\n  while (factor <= value / factor)\n    {\n      if (value % factor == 0)\n        return 0;\n      factor += increments[step];\n      step = (step + 1) & 7;\n    }\n  return 1;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n         { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while (primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n              { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0,candidateCount;\n  long int firstValue[MAXNUM];\n  long int secondValue[MAXNUM];\n  long int candidates[MAXNUM * MAXNUM];\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      firstValue[i]=atol(firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      secondValue[i]=atol(secondHalf[i]);\n\n  candidateCount=numPrimes*numPrimes;\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      { int row=i*numPrimes;\n        long int prefix=firstValue[i]*10000000L;\n        #pragma GCC ivdep\n        for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n            candidates[row+j]=prefix+secondValue[j];\n      }\n\n  for (i=0;i<candidateCount;i++)\n      { primeToTest=candidates[i];\n        if (isprime(primeToTest))\n           { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n           }\n      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n  static const int increments[8] = {4, 2, 4, 2, 4, 6, 2, 6};\n  long int factor=7;\n  int step=0;\n\n  if (value < 2)\n    return 0;\n  if (value == 2 || value == 3 || value == 5)\n    return 1;\n  if (value % 2 == 0 || value % 3 == 0 || value % 5 == 0)\n    return 0;\n\n  while (factor <= value / factor)\n    {\n      if (value % factor == 0)\n        return 0;\n      factor += increments[step];\n      step = (step + 1) & 7;\n    }\n  return 1;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n         { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while (primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n              { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0,candidateCount;\n  long int firstValue[MAXNUM];\n  long int secondValue[MAXNUM];\n  long int candidates[MAXNUM * MAXNUM];\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      firstValue[i]=atol(firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      secondValue[i]=atol(secondHalf[i]);\n\n  candidateCount=numPrimes*numPrimes;\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      { int row=i*numPrimes;\n        long int prefix=firstValue[i]*10000000L;\n        #pragma GCC ivdep\n        for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n            candidates[row+j]=prefix+secondValue[j];\n      }\n\n  for (i=0;i<candidateCount;i++)\n      { primeToTest=candidates[i];\n        if (isprime(primeToTest))\n           { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n           }\n      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n",
      "role": "user"
    }
